State: SDD

Purpose:
  Define the universal state notation used across the whole .sdd system —
  the compact symbols (+, ~, -, !, ?, >, @ and the extended =, :, *, ^, _)
  that appear in every object's own `State:` field (tasks, decisions,
  chain stages, skill runs), what each one MEANS, and how an object is
  allowed to move from one to another (see Flows below).

  This file does NOT store any object's current state value. Each object
  carries its own value inline, in its own `State:` field (same precedent
  as tasks/tasks.sdd -> Lifecycle; see Storage below). This file only
  standardizes what those values mean and how they may change.

  This file lives at .sdd root, not under project/, because state
  notation is a behavior of the .sdd system itself, not project
  knowledge (same reasoning as tasks/tasks.sdd -> Purpose). That is what
  keeps it portable: moving .sdd to a different project brings this file
  unchanged.


Owns: ./

Scope:
  State symbols
  State meanings
  State transitions
  State transition rules
  Failure states
  Blocking states
  Verification states


DoesNotOwn:
  Project state
  Task instances
  Project decisions
  Project architecture
  Skill execution

  (see architecture/architecture.sdd -> Forbidden: "STATE: MUST NOT
  define architecture." — DoesNotOwn is the concrete list form of that
  same prohibition, not a redefinition of it.)


Principle:

  State MUST describe the current condition of an SDD object.

  State MUST NOT describe the object's entire history.

  History MUST remain traceable through the object itself.


Storage:
  Unlike tasks/tasks.sdd and decisions/decisions.sdd, this file defines
  NO instance storage convention of its own. A state VALUE is not a
  separate object — it lives inline inside the `State:` field of the
  object it describes. This mirrors tasks/tasks.sdd -> Lifecycle, which
  already established that task state lives inside the task's own file,
  not in a separate global directory.

  .sdd/state/ MUST NOT contain per-object state instances — only this
  one engine file.


Symbols:

  + = DONE
  ~ = ACTIVE
  - = FAILED
  ! = BLOCKED
  ? = DECISION_REQUIRED
  > = NEXT
  @ = REFERENCE


ExtendedStates:

  = = CREATED
  : = ANALYZING
  * = REVIEW
  ^ = SUPERSEDED
  _ = ARCHIVED


Meaning:

  =: Object has been created but has not entered analysis.
  :: Object is currently being analyzed.
  >: Object is the next eligible execution target.
  ~: Object is currently being executed.
  *: Object is waiting for or undergoing review.
  +: Object successfully completed its required validation.
  -: Object failed execution or validation.
  !: Object cannot continue because a dependency, environment or
     required condition is unresolved.
  ?: Human decision is required before the object can proceed.
  ^: Object has been replaced by a newer object.
  _: Object is no longer active but is preserved for history.
  @: Reference to another SDD object.


Flows:
  (arrows written as `->` rather than a bare `>`, because `>` is itself
  the NEXT symbol — using it as the separator too would make a flow like
  `~ > > > ~` unreadable. This is a deliberate departure from the plain
  `>` chains used in tasks/tasks.sdd, made necessary by the symbol
  collision; see Note below.)

  GeneralFlow:
    = -> : -> > -> ~ -> * -> +

  FailureFlow:
    ~ -> - -> : -> > -> ~

  ReviewFailureFlow:
    * -> - -> : -> > -> ~

  BlockedFlow:
    ~ -> ! -> > -> ~

  DecisionFlow:
    ~ -> ? -> +
    ~ -> ? -> -

  SupersedeFlow:
    + -> ^ -> @ new-object


Rules:

  [S1] Every state MUST have exactly one defined meaning.
  [S2] State symbols MUST NOT be redefined by project components.
  [S3] Project-specific state MAY use these states but MUST NOT change
       their global meaning.
  [S4] AI MUST NOT skip a required state transition.
  [S5] AI MUST NOT mark an object as + without required validation.
  [S6] - MUST identify the reason for failure.
  [S7] ! MUST identify the unresolved dependency or condition.
  [S8] ? MUST identify the human decision being requested.
  [S9] ^ MUST reference the object that replaced the current object.
  [S10] _ MUST preserve the object's historical traceability.
  [S11] > MUST only be assigned to an executable or eligible next stage.
  [S12] Multiple objects MAY have ~ when they are independent and
        parallel execution is permitted.
  [S13] An object MUST NOT become ~ if a required dependency is ! or ?.
  [S14] State changes MUST be traceable.
  [S15] State represents condition, not ownership.
  [S16] Ownership MUST be resolved through the project model, not
        through state (see DoesNotOwn).


Failure:
  Every - state MUST define: reason, failed_at, failed_by, recovery_target


Recovery:
  - -> analyze failure -> identify responsible stage -> : -> > -> ~


Verification:
  An object MAY become + only when: required work completed, required
  dependencies satisfied, required reviews passed, required verification
  passed.


Escalation:
  The ? state is the point where AI MUST stop and hand control to a
  human (see PROJECT.sdd -> HumanDecision, decisions/decisions.sdd ->
  Escalation). This file does not redefine either rule — it only marks
  the state symbol that triggers them.


Navigation:
  Tasks:     @../tasks/
  Decisions: @../decisions/
  Chains:    @../chains/
  Skills:    @../skills/
  Project:   @../project/


Portability:
  State definitions MUST remain independent from project-specific data.
  Moving .sdd to another project MUST NOT require changing this file.


State: +

Note:
  (prompt/new/32.md, STEP 10) Written from the source spec with the
  following deliberate departures:

  Header renamed from the source's proposed `Spec: StateSystem` to
  `State: SDD`, matching the repo's own confirmed convention
  (`<Name>: SDD` — see tasks.sdd, decisions.sdd, architecture.sdd,
  chains.sdd, skills.sdd, prompts.sdd, and decisions.sdd's own Note log,
  which documents this pattern explicitly). `StateEngine: SDD` was also
  considered and rejected — decisions.sdd is the only sibling using an
  `<Name>Engine` form (`TaskEngine`), and even that is an outlier the
  other five siblings don't follow.

  Source's `Owns:` topic list renamed to `Scope:` — same collision and
  same fix already applied to decisions.sdd (`Owns:` is reserved for the
  directory path only, per the ExplicitReference/SingleSourceOfTruth
  precedent set there).

  `Storage:` section added (not present in source) to explicitly state
  that, unlike tasks/decisions, this file owns no instance storage
  convention at all — state values live inline in each object's own
  `State:` field. This closes a gap the source spec left implicit.

  `Escalation:` section added (not present in source) as a short
  cross-reference from `?` to PROJECT.sdd -> HumanDecision and
  decisions/decisions.sdd -> Escalation, so a reader following the state
  machine does not have to guess where `?` actually leads.

  No `Ownership:`/`Contract:`/`Types:` sections were added, even though
  decisions.sdd and tasks.sdd both have them — those sections describe
  concrete instance types those engines own. State has no instance type
  of its own (see Storage above), so those sections do not apply here.

  Source's six Flows were reformatted from a vertical one-glyph-per-line
  layout into single-line arrow chains, using `->` instead of `>` as the
  separator specifically because `>` collides with the NEXT symbol
  itself (see Flows note above). All six flows, all sixteen Rules
  ([S1]-[S16]), Failure, Recovery, Verification, Navigation and
  Portability were kept close to source — reviewed and found sound, no
  duplication with sibling files, no rejected content.

  Follow-on updates made in this same pass: PROJECT.sdd's `Directories ->
  state` and `State` sections were converted to short pointers at this
  file (NoDuplication); tasks/tasks.sdd's `States` cross-reference was
  repointed here; decisions/decisions.sdd's `States`/Note framing of `^`
  as "not part of the canonical legend" was corrected now that this file
  formally defines it as part of `ExtendedStates` (see each file's own
  Note log for the exact entry).
